`nxm` seeds a memory workspace and curates keyed text facts. Output is deterministic under the
docs determinism switch; `--json` is the machine contract (stable, declared field order).

```console
$ nxm init
initialized memory at [CWD]/.nxs
  prefix:  ab12
  modules: memory
  agent files: created AGENTS.md
  hook:    wired SessionStart → `nxm prime || cat NEXUS_MEMORY.md 2>/dev/null`
  service: registered with the background service, which keeps this workspace's deadlines

You've set up memory. nxs also ships flow and chat — run `nxs init` to add.

```

Remember facts under stable, human-chosen keys (a later remember under the same key updates in
place — bd parity), then list and recall them:

```console
$ nxm remember "auth uses JWT not sessions" --key auth-jwt --introduction "auth is JWT, not sessions"
remembered auth-jwt

$ nxm remember "always run tests with the -race flag" --key race-flag --introduction "Always run the tests with -race."
remembered race-flag

$ nxm memories
auth-jwt  auth uses JWT not sessions
race-flag  always run tests with the -race flag

$ nxm --json recall auth-jwt
{"key":"auth-jwt","body":"auth uses JWT not sessions","author":"alice","updated":"2026-06-20T10:00:00Z","active":true,"category":"unsorted","scope":"project","refs":[],"ordinal":null,"introduction":"auth is JWT, not sessions"}

```

A memory carries a **category** (which section it belongs to), a **reach** (`item`, `project` or
`global`) and the board items it is **about**. Set them while writing, or file an existing memory
afterwards — filing never touches the text, so `updated` keeps dating the fact itself:

```console
$ nxm remember "the two board levels are the load-bearing idea" --key two-levels --category architecture --scope global --refs 6j6v.5gvj,6j6v.e0z6 --introduction "the board has two levels, and that is the load-bearing idea"
remembered two-levels

$ nxm classify race-flag --category rules
classified race-flag
  category: rules
  scope:    project
  refs:     none
  intro:    Always run the tests with -race.

$ nxm memories --category rules
race-flag  always run tests with the -race flag

```

Nothing on the write path asks a model, so `nxm remember` stays offline and deterministic. Order is
a **reading** concern instead: `reorder` is the one deliberate, rarely-run verb that stores a
position, and `--ordered` reads the whole order back — the category first (`introduction`,
`architecture`, `rules`, then the rest by name, and whatever is still `unsorted` last), the stored
position within it second. A position therefore never lifts a memory out of its section, and
memories nobody has placed follow in the order they were written:

```console
$ nxm reorder two-levels auth-jwt
reordered 2 memories
  1  two-levels
  2  auth-jwt

$ nxm memories --ordered
two-levels  the two board levels are the load-bearing idea
race-flag  always run tests with the -race flag
auth-jwt  auth uses JWT not sessions

```

This is the order `nxm prime` replays at session start and `NEXUS_MEMORY.md` is generated in, so a
filed memory bestand reads front to back as the argument it was filed into.

`forget` is a reversible tombstone — the memory drops out of `memories`/`recall`:

```console
$ nxm forget race-flag
forgot race-flag

$ nxm memories
auth-jwt  auth uses JWT not sessions
two-levels  the two board levels are the load-bearing idea

```

Every one of those writes also regenerated `NEXUS_MEMORY.md` at the workspace root — the project's
memory as a **build product**, so the context survives for a reader with no nexus-flow installed
(the SessionStart hook falls back to it) and for anyone browsing the repository on a forge. It is a
projection, not a source: the store is the truth, git supplies the history. It is written in the
same reading order `nxs prime` replays — here the filed `architecture` memory leads the one still
`unsorted`. `nxm doc` prints exactly what the file holds, and `--check` is the guard that goes red
once file and store disagree:

```console
$ nxm doc
<!-- generated by nexus-flow — do not edit; run `nxm remember` instead -->
# Project memory

> **Generated file — do not edit.** Every entry below is a memory in this project's `nxm` store, projected here so the context survives without nexus-flow installed. Change it with `nxm remember` / `nxm forget`; an edit made here is overwritten by the next write. `nxm doc --check` reports drift.

> **You have already been given the index below** — all of it, or as much of it as a session start could carry. It is the same index `nxm prime` replays, which stops at the byte budget the host delivers and says so when it does (`nxm index` prints the whole of it), so there is nothing to gain by reading it again here. Underneath it stands the FULL TEXT of each memory — that is what `nxm recall <key>` serves, and it is meant to be read one memory at a time, when the index tells you a particular one matters. Reading this file end to end is the expensive way to obtain what you already have.

## Index (2)

- **two-levels**: the board has two levels, and that is the load-bearing idea
- **auth-jwt**: auth is JWT, not sessions

